eptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"candidate_mae\": 0.032779113034713904, \"combined_score\": 0.9672208869652861, \"correct\": true, \"greedy_ridge_mae\": 0.035807643760044514, \"historical_mean_mae\": 0.08984623787767475, \"observed_copy_mae\": 0.2316270572295499, \"permutation_gap\": 0.05470776022589096, \"permuted_mae\": 0.08748687326060486, \"random_lowrank_mae\": 0.0472193696884554}\n- 2026-11-12 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed quadrature-integrated posterior absolute-risk acquisition with an incumbent-inclusive shortlist and historical-only paired validation; idea_bundle_ready, no empirical improvement claim. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=The new acquisition mechanism remains an untested proposal, and aggregate prior-cycle gains do not establish a mechanism-specific recommendation or refine an existing conclusion.\n  - evidence: Recorded cycle-2 correct=true, combined_score=0.9672208869652861 and candidate_mae=0.032779113034713904 versus cycle-1 combined_score=0.9665575060124785 and candidate_mae=0.03344249398752156. No mechanism-specific ablation, component choice or paired uncertainty is supplied; cycle-1 rubric-failure cause is unknown. Named candidate source documents the finite RPC, bounded mixture medians and covariance-surrogate selection. Alternatives considered: nonlinear marginal transforms, nearest-neighbor correction and multistep planning. Selected a predictor-matched acquisition test with analytic marginal risks, independent hypothetical states, finite quadrature and Gaussian fallback. Matching JSON rubric arrays use specific executable source_contains snippets. File reads and patch writes only; no task/evaluator execution, exploratory computation, installation or web access. Only canonical local memory target: ideas/03_quadrature_bayes_risk_selection_for_latent_benchmark_mixtures/memory.md; no alternate or staging memory written.\n- 2026-11-12 | step=verify | scope=idea\n  - summary: Static bundle readback and analytic scope check complete; idea_bundle_ready. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=Static bundle checks and an analytic scope clarification add no measured evidence establishing or changing a campaign-level recommendation.\n  - evidence: Both required JSON files were read back and their rubric arrays match. One metric defers to the canonical gate; four implementation rubrics contain 5/8/5/6 executable snippets within length limits. The final bundle clarifies that the Gaussian square-root-variance identity is only an unconstrained-action reference; bounded posterior medians require the stated quadrature treatment, including for one component. No computational validator, candidate, evaluator, test or benchmark was executed. Write-set contains only one canonical idea-local memory target: ideas/03_quadrature_bayes_risk_selection_for_latent_benchmark_mixtures/memory.md; no alternate or staging memory was written.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-12T23:42:19.533527+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.9672208869652861, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"candidate_mae\": 0.032779113034713904, \"combined_score\": 0.9672208869652861, \"correct\": true, \"greedy_ridge_mae\": 0.035807643760044514, \"historical_mean_mae\": 0.08984623787767475, \"observed_copy_mae\": 0.2316270572295499, \"permutation_gap\": 0.05470776022589096, \"permuted_mae\": 0.08748687326060486, \"random_lowrank_mae\": 0.0472193696884554}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '24,155p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"nl -ba paper.tex | sed -n '24,155p'; cat references.bib\"","aggregated_output":"    24\t\n    25\tSeveral obstacles make this a prediction problem rather than a leaderboard summarization problem. Historical entries are naturally missing; absence need not be independent of model capability or evaluation choices. A high score on one task need not imply comparable scores elsewhere. Related models can make randomly held-out rows deceptively easy. Moreover, an acquisition policy determines not only which values become available but which targets remain to be scored. A method can obtain a favorable remaining-target error partly by removing difficult targets through querying. A common, never-queryable target set is therefore necessary for interpreting the comparison.\n    26\t\n    27\tWe investigate a missingness-aware latent-model procedure on frozen English MTEB score panels. Its design combines observed-data factor analysis, an optional mixture over latent profiles, conditional prediction, and transfer-oriented acquisition. Historical row-held-out validation chooses among these configurations before the outer models are queried. This is a concrete integration of established statistical tools rather than a claim of a new latent-variable architecture. The hypothesis is that modeling shared variation and benchmark-specific residuals can provide a useful conditional predictor, while accommodating heterogeneous latent profiles may help when observed scores identify specialization. The latter mechanism remains a hypothesis unless isolated by controlled evidence.\n    28\t\n    29\tThe contribution is a bounded empirical characterization with three elements. First, we specify a historical-only predictor and acquisition rule that can operate on an incomplete score matrix. Second, we compare the frozen procedure with executed random-low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines under both model-group and name-proxy-family-disjoint holdouts. Third, we make unchanged audit targets, split variation, an adverse comparison, and destroyed historical alignment central to interpretation. The measured aggregate favors the latent procedure on both target definitions, but not uniformly across splits. No additional dataset, generative-model evaluation, or independently verified lineage annotation is available. The study thus addresses when the evaluated integration is useful in this retrospective setting, not how many benchmarks are universally sufficient.\n    30\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n    31\t\n    32\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n    33\t\\centering\n    34\t\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/problem_and_evidence_overview.png}\n    35\t\\caption{Budgeted prediction and its evidence boundary. A masked historical score matrix supports fitting and internal model choice. Revealed benchmark scores condition predictions and may influence further acquisition. Outer audit columns are never queryable; only recorded target cells are scored. The common-target boundary separates predictive transfer from changes in which targets remain.}\n    36\t\\label{fig:overview}\n    37\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/program.py#handle]]\n    38\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#losses]]\n    39\t\\end{figure}\n    40\t\n    41\t\\section{Related Work}\n    42\t\\paragraph{Reducing evaluation and predicting performance.}\n    43\tItem-level compression and whole-benchmark acquisition address different units of cost. \\citet{Polo2024Tiny} study estimating benchmark performance from fewer examples; \\citet{Kipnis2024Meta} construct a sparse assessment from informative items across reasoning and knowledge benchmarks. Here a query reveals one entire benchmark-context score, not an individual test response. Observational scaling laws exploit existing model evaluations instead of training new models \\citep{Ruan2024Scaling}; skills-based scaling laws also study multi-benchmark prediction across model families \\citep{Polo2024Sloth}. Our predictor receives historical score matrices and sequentially revealed scores, without training-compute covariates. These works motivate related questions, but they are not implemented comparison methods in this study. Their generative-model settings must not be conflated with the embedding panels measured here.\n    44\t\n    45\t\\paragraph{Incomplete matrices and latent profiles.}\n    46\tMatrix factorization is a standard approach to sparse observations \\citep{Koren2009Factors}. Convex matrix completion, noisy recovery, and few-entry recovery provide complementary formulations \\citep{Candes2009Completion,Candes2010Noisy,Keshavan2010Completion}; spectral regularization offers another computational approach \\citep{Mazumder2010Spectral}. Their recovery conditions do not automatically hold for selectively reported benchmark tables. Missing-data inference requires assumptions about the observation process \\citep{Rubin1976Missing}. Our observed-data fitting uses EM-style latent sufficient statistics \\citep{Dempster1977EM}, with a benchmark-specific diagonal residual rather than the isotropic residual of probabilistic PCA \\citep{Tipping1999PPCA}.\n    47\t\n    48\tMixtures of factor analyzers supply a classical framework for combining latent factors and heterogeneous profiles \\citep{Ghahramani1996Mixture}. Related formulations study factor-mixture dimensional reduction, cluster-weighted factors, efficient conditional maximization, deeper mixtures, and incomplete high-dimensional data \\citep{Viroli2010Mixture,Subedi2012Mixture,Zhao2008ECM,Viroli2017Deep,Wei2017Missing}. The implemented extension here is narrower: shared loadings and diagonal residuals are frozen before fitting a small mixture over latent means. We neither reproduce those broader models nor establish their relative accuracy.\n    49\t\n    50\t\\paragraph{Acquisition and absolute-loss decisions.}\n    51\tBayesian experimental design and statistical active learning connect acquisition to posterior uncertainty and prediction objectives \\citep{Chaloner1995Design,Cohn1996Active}. Absolute loss calls for a median rather than a mean under the predictive distribution; quantile regression and forecast assessment emphasize matching the action to the loss \\citep{Koenker2001Quantiles,Gneiting2011Forecasts}. This motivates bounded posterior medians and a square-root-variance transfer heuristic. Loss alignment at prediction time does not make the acquisition heuristic an exact expected-risk optimizer, especially under a mixture posterior. Our contribution is the measured integration and its limits, not novelty of these statistical principles.\n    52\t\n    53\t\\section{Method}\n    54\t\\subsection{Problem Setup and Evaluation Target}\n    55\tLet $X\\in[0,1]^{M\\times N}$ contain historical scores and let $R$ be their binary observation mask. A row denotes an embedding model; a column denotes a benchmark together with language, evaluation split, primary metric, and dataset revision. An unrecorded cell is missing, not a zero score. For a held-out model with score vector $y$, the evaluator exposes an available query set $A$. The policy selects distinct indices $S_k\\subseteq A$ and observes $y_{S_k}$ under a budget $k$. Its prediction $\\widehat y^{(k)}$ is evaluated only where a target score is recorded.\n    56\t\n    57\tWrite $O$ for the recorded target columns and $H$ for the audit columns, with $A=O\\setminus H$. The two losses are\n    58\t\\begin{equation}\n    59\t L_{\\rm rem}^{(k)}=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in O\\setminus S_k}|\\widehat y_j^{(k)}-y_j|}{|O\\setminus S_k|},\n    60\t \\qquad\n    61\t L_{\\rm audit}^{(k)}=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in O\\cap H}|\\widehat y_j^{(k)}-y_j|}{|O\\cap H|}.\n    62\t \\label{eq:losses}\n    63\t\\end{equation}\n    64\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#losses]]\n    65\tRemaining-target loss measures the practical residual prediction task but has method-dependent targets. Audit loss holds the scored cells fixed across methods and budgets within each held-out row. Both losses are needed: the former describes the acquisition-dependent task, while the latter tests prediction without removing difficult audit targets. Neither evaluates cells that were never measured in the public table.\n    66\t\n    67\t\\subsection{Design Rationale and Observed-Data Fitting}\n    68\tThe central design hypothesis is that shared cross-benchmark variation can be estimated without treating absent measurements as observed outcomes. We fit a Gaussian factor model\n    69\t\\begin{equation}\n    70\t x=b+Wz+\\epsilon,\\qquad z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I),\\quad\n    71\t \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,D),\\quad \\Sigma=WW^\\top+D,\n    72\t \\label{eq:factor}\n    73\t\\end{equation}\n    74\twhere $D$ is diagonal. Its entries represent benchmark-specific variation across models, not repeated-measurement noise. Historical means and scales are computed from recorded training entries. A zero-filled standardized matrix initializes the loadings by SVD, but subsequent likelihood contributions and sufficient statistics use only observed coordinates. This distinction matters: initialization does not turn zeros in missing cells into training labels.\n    75\t\n    76\tFor historical row $i$ with observed coordinates $O_i$, the latent posterior has covariance and mean\n    77\t\\begin{equation}\n    78\t V_i=(I+W_{O_i}^{\\top}D_{O_i}^{-1}W_{O_i})^{-1},\\qquad\n    79\t m_i=V_iW_{O_i}^{\\top}D_{O_i}^{-1}(x_{i,O_i}-b_{O_i}).\n    80\t \\label{eq:posterior}\n    81\t\\end{equation}\n    82\tThe loading and residual updates integrate $\\mathbb E[zz^\\top\\mid x_{i,O_i}]=V_i+m_im_i^\\top$. Each benchmark's update uses rows where that benchmark is recorded. Diagonal regularization stabilizes the linear solves; shrinkage and a variance floor prevent nearly singular residuals. This models the observed score distribution without supplying a model for why particular leaderboard entries are missing.\n    83\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#ResidualCovariance.fit]]\n    84\t\n    85\t\\subsection{Conditional Prediction and Heterogeneous Profiles}\n    86\tA single Gaussian gives a clipped conditional mean,\n    87\t\\begin{equation}\n    88\t \\widehat y=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}\n    89\t \\left[b+\\Sigma_{:,S}(\\Sigma_{S,S}+\\delta I)^{-1}(y_S-b_S)\\right],\n    90\t \\label{eq:gaussian}\n    91\t\\end{equation}\n    92\twith queried coordinates restored to their observed values. For this symmetric conditional distribution, the clipped mean is also a constrained median. The mixture extension retains $b,W,D$ and replaces the latent prior by\n    93\t\\begin{equation}\n    94\t p(z)=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\pi_g\\mathcal N(z;a_g,\\tau^2I),\\qquad\n    95\t \\mu_g=b+Wa_g,\\qquad C=\\tau^2WW^\\top+D.\n    96\t \\label{eq:mixture}\n    97\t\\end{equation}\n    98\tThe single-component alternative is the original Gaussian, not a separately fitted approximation. For multiple components, observed-data EM fits the masses, latent centers, and shared latent variance with regularization. Each revealed score updates component probabilities, conditional means, and their shared within-component covariance. Distinct target rows receive independent conditional states.\n    99\t\n   100\tFor each unqueried benchmark the mixture predictor approximately solves\n   101\t$\\sum_g\\pi_g\\Phi((a-\\mu_{g,j})/\\sqrt{C_{jj}})=1/2$ on $a\\in[0,1]$ by bisection, choosing a boundary if the unconstrained median is outside the interval. This is the constrained Bayes action for modeled absolute error: where differentiable, the derivative of $\\mathbb E|Y-a|$ is $2F_Y(a)-1$. The argument concerns the fitted predictive distribution, not the true distribution of new embedding models. It supplies no real-data optimality or uncertainty-calibration guarantee.\n   102\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#LatentMixture.fit_profiles]]\n   103\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.reveal]]\n   104\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.predict]]\n   105\t\n   106\t\\subsection{Transfer-Oriented Acquisition}\n   107\tLet $T$ be the current modeled covariance, including between-component variation when a mixture is used. For candidate $q\\in A\\setminus S$, the variance-transfer score is\n   108\t\\begin{equation}\n   109\t U_{\\rm var}(q)=\\sum_{j\\notin S\\cup\\{q\\}}\\frac{T_{jq}^2}{\\max(T_{qq},\\delta)}.\n   110\t \\label{eq:transfer}\n   111\t\\end{equation}\n   112\tFor a Gaussian, this sums the conditional variance reductions on other columns. Excluding $q$ prevents a benchmark from receiving credit merely for revealing its own score. All modeled unobserved columns enter this utility, including columns unavailable for querying; the policy is not given the outer audit labels. Consequently its utility is not identical to either scored loss in Equation~\\ref{eq:losses}.\n   113\t\n   114\tA second selectable criterion replaces variance reduction with the sum of square-root reductions,\n   115\t\\begin{equation}\n   116\t U_{\\rm sqrt}(q)=\\sum_{j\\notin S\\cup\\{q\\}}\n   117\t \\left[\\sqrt{T_{jj}}-\n   118\t \\sqrt{\\max\\{T_{jj}-T_{jq}^2/\\max(T_{qq},\\delta),0\\}}\\right].\n   119\t \\label{eq:sqrt}\n   120\t\\end{equation}\n   121\tThe motivation is the relation between Gaussian absolute risk and standard deviation. Under a mixture, the total covariance is moment matched and depends on revealed values through posterior component weights. Equation~\\ref{eq:sqrt} is nevertheless a heuristic: it does not integrate over possible queried values or recompute the expected bounded-mixture median risk. A single Gaussian's acquisition is score-value independent, although its predictions are not. We do not infer that value-dependent acquisition was used in every evaluated fit.\n   122\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#transfer_select]]\n   123\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.select]]\n   124\t\n   125\t\\subsection{Historical Model Choice and Compared Pipelines}\n   126\tThe complete procedure, denoted \\latent, chooses rank and residual shrinkage using a historical row split with fixed internal audit columns. It compares the Gaussian with a small set of mixture sizes, regularization strengths, and the two acquisition criteria. A configuration replaces the Gaussian only if its internal audit error improves beyond a margin based on paired historical-row differences. It is refitted on all supplied historical rows; insufficient validation or numerical failure falls back to the Gaussian. These are executable safeguards, not externally measured component ablations. The outer evidence does not establish which component counts, selectors, or fallbacks were realized, and internally reported diagnostics are not independently verified.\n   127\t\n   128\t\\randomlr\\ uses random acquisition and a masked alternating-ridge low-rank predictor, with historical cell-held-out tuning. \\ridge\\ greedily covers squared historical correlations, shrunk according to pairwise overlap, and predicts each target by ridge regression on the selected scores. Its masked, centered historical predictors are zero at missing coordinates. The historical-mean reference predicts each benchmark's recorded training mean. Unlike matched acquisition ablations, these comparisons vary both acquisition and prediction. Any measured advantage belongs to the complete evaluated configuration, not solely to residual modeling, mixtures, medians, or the acquisition criterion. Supplementary Information gives source locations and numerical settings.\n   129\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#tune_fit]]\n   130\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#native_random]]\n   131\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#native_ridge]]\n   132\t\n   133\t\\section{Experimental Setup}\n   134\t\\paragraph{Data and scope.}\n   135\tThe frozen source is the public MTEB results repository at revision \\texttt{6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383}. Each evaluation panel contains 48 benchmark-context columns and 48 held-out rows, with natural missingness retained. Classification accuracy, clustering V-measure, retrieval NDCG, and reranking MAP are treated on their declared unit intervals; signed Spearman correlations are mapped by $(s+1)/2$. Dataset revisions remain part of column identity. No imputed cell is a target, and no held-out extrema determine normalization. Historical coverage determines panel membership. Held-out row inclusion depends on recorded availability, not observed error. This is an embedding-model study on English panels, not an evaluation of generative language models or all tasks in the upstream repository.\n   136\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   137\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json]]\n   138\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#score_cells]]\n   139\t\n   140\t\\paragraph{Holdouts and controls.}\n   141\tModel-group holdouts separate identity components defined using normalized source content. The stricter condition additionally separates name-defined family proxies between historical and held-out rows. These proxies are not independently verified model genealogies. Frozen development and final pools are separated using normalized document and complete-row content checks; within each final split, the evaluator rechecks component and document disjointness. This reduces a specific form of duplication, not every possible form of evaluation contamination.\n   142\t\n   143\tThe final split seeds are 4001, 4009, and 4021. Query checkpoints are $k\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}$, and eight columns are never queryable audit columns. Only recorded audit cells are scored; not every held-out row has all audit measurements. Query availability is its recorded mask minus these columns, an explicitly retrospective constraint. A column-wise permutation control shuffles scores among recorded historical positions, preserving masks and each column's marginal values while disrupting cross-task alignment. Models and internal choices are refitted on the transformed history. A required deterministic reset replay is retained as a protocol diagnostic but receives no weight in accuracy summaries.\n   144\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n   145\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json#/production]]\n   146\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#permute_history]]\n   147\t\n   148\t\\paragraph{Aggregation and uncertainty.}\n   149\tWe average checkpoints equally within a row. Model-group summaries weight rows equally; family-disjoint summaries first average rows within a proxy family and then weight families equally. The final aggregate weights each original, non-replay condition--seed case equally. Historical-mean remaining-target errors use \\latent's remaining sets; the common-audit mean does not depend on its selections. The final evaluation completed and met its operational qualification checks. This status is separate from predictive competitiveness and cannot replace scientific qualification of a broader claim.\n   150\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#row_average]]\n   151\t% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#plane_metrics]]\n   152\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status/value]]\n   153\t% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome/value]]\n   154\t\n   155\tThe seeds define frozen split realizations, not independent population draws or repeated fits to one identical test set. Rows and families can recur across splits. We therefore show raw split values and descriptive means, without row-level pseudo-replication, significance tests, or confidence intervals presented as independent-population uncertainty. Model selection used historical data only; the final measurements are reported separately from development scores. No additional evaluations were run for this manuscript.\n@article{Candes2009Completion,\n  author = {Emmanuel J. Candès and Benjamin Recht},\n  title = {{Exact Matrix Completion via Convex Optimization}},\n  year = {2009},\n  journal = {Foundations of Computational Mathematics}\n}\n\n@article{Candes2010Noisy,\n  author = {Emmanuel J Candes and Yaniv Plan},\n  title = {{Matrix Completion With Noise}},\n  year = {2010},\n  journal = {Proceedings of the IEEE},\n  doi = {10.1109/jproc.2009.2035722}\n}\n\n@article{Chaloner1995Design,\n  author = {Kathryn Chaloner and Isabella Verdinelli},\n  title = {{Bayesian Experimental Design: A Review}},\n  year = {1995},\n  journal = {Statistical Science},\n  doi = {10.1214/ss/1177009939}\n}\n\n@article{Cohn1996Active,\n  author = {D. A. Cohn and Z. Ghahramani and M. I. Jordan},\n  title = {{Active Learning with Statistical Models}},\n  year = {1996},\n  journal = {Journal of Artificial Intelligence Research},\n  doi = {10.1613/jair.295}\n}\n\n@article{Dempster1977EM,\n  author = {A. P. Dempster and N. M. Laird and D. B. Rubin},\n  title = {{Maximum Likelihood from Incomplete Data Via the EM Algorithm}},\n  year = {1977},\n  journal = {Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology},\n  doi = {10.1111/j.2517-6161.1977.tb01600.x}\n}\n\n@article{Ghahramani1996Mixture,\n  author = {Zoubin Ghahramani and Geoffrey E. Hinton},\n  title = {{The EM algorithm for mixtures of factor analyzers}},\n  year = {1996},\n  journal = {Technical report}\n}\n\n@article{Gneiting2011Forecasts,\n  author = {Gneiting, Tilmann},\n  title = {{Making and Evaluating Point Forecasts}},\n  year = {2009},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:0912.0902}\n}\n\n@article{Hendrycks2021MMLU,\n  author = {Hendrycks, Dan and Burns, Collin and Basart, Steven and Zou, Andy and Mazeika, Mantas and Song, Dawn and Steinhardt, Jacob},\n  title = {{Measuring Massive Multitask Language Understanding}},\n  year = {2020},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2009.03300}\n}\n\n@article{Keshavan2010Completion,\n  author = {Raghunandan H. Keshavan and Andrea Montanari and Sewoong Oh},\n  title = {{Matrix Completion From a Few Entries}},\n  year = {2010},\n  journal = {IEEE Transactions on Information Theory},\n  doi = {10.1109/tit.2010.2046205}\n}\n\n@article{Kipnis2024Meta,\n  author = {Alex Kipnis and Konstantinos Voudouris and Luca M. Schulze Buschoff and Eric Schulz},\n  title = {{metabench -- A Sparse Benchmark of Reasoning and Knowledge in Large Language Models}},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2407.12844}\n}\n\n@article{Koenker2001Quantiles,\n  author = {Roger Koenker and Kevin F Hallock},\n  title = {{Quantile Regression}},\n  year = {2001},\n  journal = {Journal of Economic Perspectives},\n  doi = {10.1257/jep.15.4.143}\n}\n\n@article{Koren2009Factors,\n  author = {Yehuda Koren and Robert Bell and Chris Volinsky},\n  title = {{Matrix Factorization Techniques for Recommender Systems}},\n  year = {2009},\n  journal = {Computer},\n  doi = {10.1109/mc.2009.263}\n}\n\n@article{Liang2023HELM,\n  author = {Liang, Percy and Bommasani, Rishi and Lee, Tony and Tsipras, Dimitris and Soylu, Dilara and Yasunaga, Michihiro and Zhang, Yian and Narayanan, Deepak and Wu, Yuhuai and Kumar, Ananya and Newman, Benjamin and Yuan, Binhang and Yan, Bobby and Zhang, Ce and Cosgrove, Christian and Manning, Christopher D. and Ré, Christopher and Acosta-Navas, Diana and Hudson, Drew A. and Zelikman, Eric and Durmus, Esin and Ladhak, Faisal and Rong, Frieda and Ren, Hongyu and Yao, Huaxiu and Wang, Jue and Santhanam, Keshav and Orr, Laurel and Zheng, Lucia and Yuksekgonul, Mert and Suzgun, Mirac and Kim, Nathan and Guha, Neel and Chatterji, Niladri and Khattab, Omar and Henderson, Peter and Huang, Qian and Chi, Ryan and Xie, Sang Michael and Santurkar, Shibani and Ganguli, Surya and Hashimoto, Tatsunori and Icard, Thomas and Zhang, Tianyi and Chaudhary, Vishrav and Wang, William and Li, Xuechen and Mai, Yifan and Zhang, Yuhui and Koreeda, Yuta},\n  title = {{Holistic Evaluation of Language Models}},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2211.09110}\n}\n\n@article{Mazumder2010Spectral,\n  author = {Rahul Mazumder and Trevor Hastie and Robert Tibshirani},\n  title = {{Spectral Regularization Algorithms for Learning Large Incomplete Matrices}},\n  year = {2010},\n  journal = {Journal of Machine Learning Research},\n  volume = {11},\n  pages = {2287--2322}\n}\n\n@article{Muennighoff2023MTEB,\n  author = {Muennighoff, Niklas and Tazi, Nouamane and Magne, Loïc and Reimers, Nils},\n  title = {{MTEB: Massive Text Embedding Benchmark}},\n  year = {2022},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2210.07316}\n}\n\n@article{Polo2024Sloth,\n  author = {F. Polo and Seamus Somerstep and Leshem Choshen and Yue-Kai Sun and Mikhail Yurochkin},\n  title = {{Sloth: scaling laws for LLM skills to predict multi-benchmark performance across families}},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2412.06540},\n  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2412.06540}\n}\n\n@article{Polo2024Tiny,\n  author = {Polo, Felipe Maia and Weber, Lucas and Choshen, Leshem and Sun, Yuekai and Xu, Gongjun and Yurochkin, Mikhail},\n  title = {{tinyBenchmarks: evaluating LLMs with fewer examples}},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2402.14992}\n}\n\n@article{Ruan2024Scaling,\n  author = {Yangjun Ruan and Chris J. Maddison and Tatsunori B. Hashimoto},\n  title = {{Observational Scaling Laws and the Predictability of Language Model Performance}},\n  year = {2024},\n  journal = {arXiv preprint arXiv:2405.10938},\n  doi = {10.48550/arXiv.2405.10938}\n}\n\n@article{Rubin1976Missing,\n  author = {DONALD B. RUBIN},\n  title = {{Inference and missing data}},\n  year = {1976},\n  journal = {Biometrika},\n  doi = {10.1093/biomet/63.3.581}\n}\n\n@article{Subedi2012Mixture,\n  author = {Sanjeena Subedi and Antonio Punzo and Salvatore Ingrassia and Paul D. McNicholas},\n  title = {{Clustering and classification via cluster-weighted factor analyzers}},\n  year = {2013},\n  journal = {Advances in Data Analysis and Classification},\n  doi = {10.1007/s11634-013-0124-8}\n}\n\n@article{Tipping1999PPCA,\n  author = {Michael E. Tipping and Christopher M. Bishop},\n  title = {{Probabilistic Principal Component Analysis}},\n  year = {1999},\n  journal = {Journal of the Royal Statistical Society Series B: Statistical Methodology},\n  doi = {10.1111/1467-9868.00196}\n}\n\n@article{Viroli2010Mixture,\n  author = {Cinzia Viroli},\n  title = {{Dimensionally Reduced Model-Based Clustering Through Mixtures of Factor Mixture Analyzers}},\n  year = {2010},\n  journal = {Journal of Classification},\n  doi = {10.1007/s00357-010-9063-7}\n}\n\n@article{Viroli2017Deep,\n  author = {Cinzia Viroli and Geoffrey J. McLachlan},\n  title = {{Deep Gaussian mixture models}},\n  year = {2019},\n  journal = {Statistics and Computing},\n  doi = {10.1007/s11222-017-9793-z}\n}\n\n@article{Wei2017Missing,\n  author = {Yuhong Wei and Yang Tang and Paul D. McNicholas},\n  title = {{Flexible High-Dimensional Unsupervised Learning with Missing Data}},\n  year = {2020},\n  journal = {IEEE Transactions on Pattern Analysis and Machine Intelligence},\n  doi = {10.1109/tpami.2018.2885760}\n}\n\n@article{Zhao2008ECM,\n  author = {Jian-Hua Zhao and P.L.H. Yu},\n  title = {{Fast ML Estimation for the Mixture of Factor Analyzers via an ECM Algorithm}},\n  year = {2008},\n  journal = {IEEE Transactions on Neural Networks},\n  doi = {10.1109/tnn.2008.2003467}\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
