{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '41,112p' paper.tex\"","aggregated_output":"\\section{Related Work}\n\\paragraph{Reducing evaluation and predicting performance.}\nItem-level compression and whole-benchmark acquisition address different units of cost. \\citet{Polo2024Tiny} study estimating benchmark performance from fewer examples; \\citet{Kipnis2024Meta} construct a sparse assessment from informative items across reasoning and knowledge benchmarks. Here a query reveals one entire benchmark-context score, not an individual test response. Observational scaling laws exploit existing model evaluations instead of training new models \\citep{Ruan2024Scaling}; skills-based scaling laws also study multi-benchmark prediction across model families \\citep{Polo2024Sloth}. Our predictor receives historical score matrices and sequentially revealed scores, without training-compute covariates. These works motivate related questions, but they are not implemented comparison methods in this study. Their generative-model settings must not be conflated with the embedding panels measured here.\n\n\\paragraph{Incomplete matrices and latent profiles.}\nMatrix factorization is a standard approach to sparse observations \\citep{Koren2009Factors}. Convex matrix completion, noisy recovery, and few-entry recovery provide complementary formulations \\citep{Candes2009Completion,Candes2010Noisy,Keshavan2010Completion}; spectral regularization offers another computational approach \\citep{Mazumder2010Spectral}. Their recovery conditions do not automatically hold for selectively reported benchmark tables. Missing-data inference requires assumptions about the observation process \\citep{Rubin1976Missing}. Our observed-data fitting uses EM-style latent sufficient statistics \\citep{Dempster1977EM}, with a benchmark-specific diagonal residual rather than the isotropic residual of probabilistic PCA \\citep{Tipping1999PPCA}.\n\nMixtures of factor analyzers supply a classical framework for combining latent factors and heterogeneous profiles \\citep{Ghahramani1996Mixture}. Related formulations study factor-mixture dimensional reduction, cluster-weighted factors, efficient conditional maximization, deeper mixtures, and incomplete high-dimensional data \\citep{Viroli2010Mixture,Subedi2012Mixture,Zhao2008ECM,Viroli2017Deep,Wei2017Missing}. The implemented extension here is narrower: shared loadings and diagonal residuals are frozen before fitting a small mixture over latent means. We neither reproduce those broader models nor establish their relative accuracy.\n\n\\paragraph{Acquisition and absolute-loss decisions.}\nBayesian experimental design and statistical active learning connect acquisition to posterior uncertainty and prediction objectives \\citep{Chaloner1995Design,Cohn1996Active}. Absolute loss calls for a median rather than a mean under the predictive distribution; quantile regression and forecast assessment emphasize matching the action to the loss \\citep{Koenker2001Quantiles,Gneiting2011Forecasts}. This motivates bounded posterior medians and a square-root-variance transfer heuristic. Loss alignment at prediction time does not make the acquisition heuristic an exact expected-risk optimizer, especially under a mixture posterior. Our contribution is the measured integration and its limits, not novelty of these statistical principles.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\\subsection{Problem Setup and Evaluation Target}\nLet $X\\in[0,1]^{M\\times N}$ contain historical scores and let $R$ be their binary observation mask. A row denotes an embedding model; a column denotes a benchmark together with language, evaluation split, primary metric, and dataset revision. An unrecorded cell is missing, not a zero score. For a held-out model with score vector $y$, the evaluator exposes an available query set $A$. The policy selects distinct indices $S_k\\subseteq A$ and observes $y_{S_k}$ under a budget $k$. Its prediction $\\widehat y^{(k)}$ is evaluated only where a target score is recorded.\n\nWrite $O$ for the recorded target columns and $H$ for the audit columns, with $A=O\\setminus H$. The two losses are\n\\begin{equation}\n L_{\\rm rem}^{(k)}=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in O\\setminus S_k}|\\widehat y_j^{(k)}-y_j|}{|O\\setminus S_k|},\n \\qquad\n L_{\\rm audit}^{(k)}=\\frac{\\sum_{j\\in O\\cap H}|\\widehat y_j^{(k)}-y_j|}{|O\\cap H|}.\n \\label{eq:losses}\n\\end{equation}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#losses]]\nRemaining-target loss measures the practical residual prediction task but has method-dependent targets. Audit loss holds the scored cells fixed across methods and budgets within each held-out row. Both losses are needed: the former describes the acquisition-dependent task, while the latter tests prediction without removing difficult audit targets. Neither evaluates cells that were never measured in the public table.\n\n\\subsection{Design Rationale and Observed-Data Fitting}\nThe central design hypothesis is that shared cross-benchmark variation can be estimated without treating absent measurements as observed outcomes. We fit a Gaussian factor model\n\\begin{equation}\n x=b+Wz+\\epsilon,\\qquad z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I),\\quad\n \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,D),\\quad \\Sigma=WW^\\top+D,\n \\label{eq:factor}\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $D$ is diagonal. Its entries represent benchmark-specific variation across models, not repeated-measurement noise. Historical means and scales are computed from recorded training entries. A zero-filled standardized matrix initializes the loadings by SVD, but subsequent likelihood contributions and sufficient statistics use only observed coordinates. This distinction matters: initialization does not turn zeros in missing cells into training labels.\n\nFor historical row $i$ with observed coordinates $O_i$, the latent posterior has covariance and mean\n\\begin{equation}\n V_i=(I+W_{O_i}^{\\top}D_{O_i}^{-1}W_{O_i})^{-1},\\qquad\n m_i=V_iW_{O_i}^{\\top}D_{O_i}^{-1}(x_{i,O_i}-b_{O_i}).\n \\label{eq:posterior}\n\\end{equation}\nThe loading and residual updates integrate $\\mathbb E[zz^\\top\\mid x_{i,O_i}]=V_i+m_im_i^\\top$. Each benchmark's update uses rows where that benchmark is recorded. Diagonal regularization stabilizes the linear solves; shrinkage and a variance floor prevent nearly singular residuals. This models the observed score distribution without supplying a model for why particular leaderboard entries are missing.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#ResidualCovariance.fit]]\n\n\\subsection{Conditional Prediction and Heterogeneous Profiles}\nA single Gaussian gives a clipped conditional mean,\n\\begin{equation}\n \\widehat y=\\operatorname{clip}_{[0,1]}\n \\left[b+\\Sigma_{:,S}(\\Sigma_{S,S}+\\delta I)^{-1}(y_S-b_S)\\right],\n \\label{eq:gaussian}\n\\end{equation}\nwith queried coordinates restored to their observed values. For this symmetric conditional distribution, the clipped mean is also a constrained median. The mixture extension retains $b,W,D$ and replaces the latent prior by\n\\begin{equation}\n p(z)=\\sum_{g=1}^{G}\\pi_g\\mathcal N(z;a_g,\\tau^2I),\\qquad\n \\mu_g=b+Wa_g,\\qquad C=\\tau^2WW^\\top+D.\n \\label{eq:mixture}\n\\end{equation}\nThe single-component alternative is the original Gaussian, not a separately fitted approximation. For multiple components, observed-data EM fits the masses, latent centers, and shared latent variance with regularization. Each revealed score updates component probabilities, conditional means, and their shared within-component covariance. Distinct target rows receive independent conditional states.\n\nFor each unqueried benchmark the mixture predictor approximately solves\n$\\sum_g\\pi_g\\Phi((a-\\mu_{g,j})/\\sqrt{C_{jj}})=1/2$ on $a\\in[0,1]$ by bisection, choosing a boundary if the unconstrained median is outside the interval. This is the constrained Bayes action for modeled absolute error: where differentiable, the derivative of $\\mathbb E|Y-a|$ is $2F_Y(a)-1$. The argument concerns the fitted predictive distribution, not the true distribution of new embedding models. It supplies no real-data optimality or uncertainty-calibration guarantee.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#LatentMixture.fit_profiles]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.reveal]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.predict]]\n\n\\subsection{Transfer-Oriented Acquisition}\nLet $T$ be the current modeled covariance, including between-component variation when a mixture is used. For candidate $q\\in A\\setminus S$, the variance-transfer score is\n\\begin{equation}\n U_{\\rm var}(q)=\\sum_{j\\notin S\\cup\\{q\\}}\\frac{T_{jq}^2}{\\max(T_{qq},\\delta)}.\n \\label{eq:transfer}\n\\end{equation}\nFor a Gaussian, this sums the conditional variance reductions on other columns. Excluding $q$ prevents a benchmark from receiving credit merely for revealing its own score. All modeled unobserved columns enter this utility, including columns unavailable for querying; the policy is not given the outer audit labels. Consequently its utility is not identical to either scored loss in Equation~\\ref{eq:losses}.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '113,210p' paper.tex; sed -n '255,314p' paper.tex; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"sed -n '113,210p' paper.tex; sed -n '255,314p' paper.tex; cat global_memory_summary.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nA second selectable criterion replaces variance reduction with the sum of square-root reductions,\n\\begin{equation}\n U_{\\rm sqrt}(q)=\\sum_{j\\notin S\\cup\\{q\\}}\n \\left[\\sqrt{T_{jj}}-\n \\sqrt{\\max\\{T_{jj}-T_{jq}^2/\\max(T_{qq},\\delta),0\\}}\\right].\n \\label{eq:sqrt}\n\\end{equation}\nThe motivation is the relation between Gaussian absolute risk and standard deviation. Under a mixture, the total covariance is moment matched and depends on revealed values through posterior component weights. Equation~\\ref{eq:sqrt} is nevertheless a heuristic: it does not integrate over possible queried values or recompute the expected bounded-mixture median risk. A single Gaussian's acquisition is score-value independent, although its predictions are not. We do not infer that value-dependent acquisition was used in every evaluated fit.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#transfer_select]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.select]]\n\n\\subsection{Historical Model Choice and Compared Pipelines}\nThe complete procedure, denoted \\latent, chooses rank and residual shrinkage using a historical row split with fixed internal audit columns. It compares the Gaussian with a small set of mixture sizes, regularization strengths, and the two acquisition criteria. A configuration replaces the Gaussian only if its internal audit error improves beyond a margin based on paired historical-row differences. It is refitted on all supplied historical rows; insufficient validation or numerical failure falls back to the Gaussian. These are executable safeguards, not externally measured component ablations. The outer evidence does not establish which component counts, selectors, or fallbacks were realized, and internally reported diagnostics are not independently verified.\n\n\\randomlr\\ uses random acquisition and a masked alternating-ridge low-rank predictor, with historical cell-held-out tuning. \\ridge\\ greedily covers squared historical correlations, shrunk according to pairwise overlap, and predicts each target by ridge regression on the selected scores. Its masked, centered historical predictors are zero at missing coordinates. The historical-mean reference predicts each benchmark's recorded training mean. Unlike matched acquisition ablations, these comparisons vary both acquisition and prediction. Any measured advantage belongs to the complete evaluated configuration, not solely to residual modeling, mixtures, medians, or the acquisition criterion. Supplementary Information gives source locations and numerical settings.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#tune_fit]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#native_random]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#native_ridge]]\n\n\\section{Experimental Setup}\n\\paragraph{Data and scope.}\nThe frozen source is the public MTEB results repository at revision \\texttt{6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383}. Each evaluation panel contains 48 benchmark-context columns and 48 held-out rows, with natural missingness retained. Classification accuracy, clustering V-measure, retrieval NDCG, and reranking MAP are treated on their declared unit intervals; signed Spearman correlations are mapped by $(s+1)/2$. Dataset revisions remain part of column identity. No imputed cell is a target, and no held-out extrema determine normalization. Historical coverage determines panel membership. Held-out row inclusion depends on recorded availability, not observed error. This is an embedding-model study on English panels, not an evaluation of generative language models or all tasks in the upstream repository.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#score_cells]]\n\n\\paragraph{Holdouts and controls.}\nModel-group holdouts separate identity components defined using normalized source content. The stricter condition additionally separates name-defined family proxies between historical and held-out rows. These proxies are not independently verified model genealogies. Frozen development and final pools are separated using normalized document and complete-row content checks; within each final split, the evaluator rechecks component and document disjointness. This reduces a specific form of duplication, not every possible form of evaluation contamination.\n\nThe final split seeds are 4001, 4009, and 4021. Query checkpoints are $k\\in\\{1,2,4,8\\}$, and eight columns are never queryable audit columns. Only recorded audit cells are scored; not every held-out row has all audit measurements. Query availability is its recorded mask minus these columns, an explicitly retrospective constraint. A column-wise permutation control shuffles scores among recorded historical positions, preserving masks and each column's marginal values while disrupting cross-task alignment. Models and internal choices are refitted on the transformed history. A required deterministic reset replay is retained as a protocol diagnostic but receives no weight in accuracy summaries.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/evaluation_evidence/episodes]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json#/production]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#permute_history]]\n\n\\paragraph{Aggregation and uncertainty.}\nWe average checkpoints equally within a row. Model-group summaries weight rows equally; family-disjoint summaries first average rows within a proxy family and then weight families equally. The final aggregate weights each original, non-replay condition--seed case equally. Historical-mean remaining-target errors use \\latent's remaining sets; the common-audit mean does not depend on its selections. The final evaluation completed and met its operational qualification checks. This status is separate from predictive competitiveness and cannot replace scientific qualification of a broader claim.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#row_average]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#plane_metrics]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.status/value]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/grounding_sources/final.qualification.outcome/value]]\n\nThe seeds define frozen split realizations, not independent population draws or repeated fits to one identical test set. Rows and families can recur across splits. We therefore show raw split values and descriptive means, without row-level pseudo-replication, significance tests, or confidence intervals presented as independent-population uncertainty. Model selection used historical data only; the final measurements are reported separately from development scores. No additional evaluations were run for this manuscript.\n\n\\section{Results}\n\\subsection{Aggregate Accuracy on Remaining and Common Targets}\nTable~\\ref{tab:main} reports the frozen comparison. \\latent\\ has lower aggregate error than both executed predictive pipelines on the remaining-target objective. However, remaining sets differ between policies. The audit comparison is therefore essential: \\latent's common-target error is also lower, showing that its aggregate advantage is not confined to the acquisition-dependent target definition. The absolute values are normalized score errors; a unit-normalized accuracy difference and a transformed correlation difference do not have identical application-level meanings.\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Frozen aggregate normalized MAE; lower is better. Audit cells are never queryable. Means equally weight condition--seed cases after the prescribed row/family aggregation. These are descriptive comparisons, not significance claims.}\n\\label{tab:main}\n\\begin{tabular}{lrr}\n\\toprule\nPipeline & Remaining targets & Common audit targets \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\latent & 0.03130 & 0.02687 \\\\\n\\ridge & 0.03607 & 0.02941 \\\\\n\\randomlr & 0.04563 & 0.03547 \\\\\nHistorical mean & 0.08782 & 0.06056 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/aggregate_original]]\n\\end{table}\n\nThe historical mean is substantially less accurate in aggregate on both target definitions, indicating that a benchmark-specific constant is insufficient for these evaluated panels. This does not establish the value of any particular component of \\latent: conditional predictors with different fitting and selection choices also improve over the mean. Nor does the aggregate measure prediction of naturally unrecorded cells, for which no truth is available. The smaller audit errors should not be interpreted as intrinsically easier prediction by the same numerical factor: the fixed audit columns form a different, coverage-selected target set.\n\n\\subsection{Budget Trade-Offs on Unchanged Targets}\nFigure~\\ref{fig:budget} holds audit cells unchanged as the query budget increases. For model-group holdouts, \\latent's descriptive mean audit MAE decreases from 0.03286 at one query to 0.01700 at eight; the corresponding \\ridge\\ values are 0.03411 and 0.02156. For family-disjoint holdouts, \\latent\\ decreases from 0.03880 to 0.02084, compared with 0.04084 to 0.02273 for \\ridge. The family-disjoint mean at two queries favors \\ridge\\ (0.03153 versus 0.03363), so even the budget-specific ordering is not uniform. Additional observations help the descriptive mean within the measured range, but no predefined acceptable-error threshold or universal sufficient budget was tested.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/audit_budget_curves]]\n\n\\begin{figure}[t]\n\\centering\n\\begin{subfigure}{0.487\\linewidth}\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/common_target_budget_comparison_model_group.pdf}\n\\caption{Model-group holdout}\n\\end{subfigure}\\hfill\n\\begin{subfigure}{0.487\\linewidth}\n\\includegraphics[width=\\linewidth]{figures/common_target_budget_comparison_family_disjoint.pdf}\n\\caption{Name-proxy-family-disjoint holdout}\n\\end{subfigure}\n\\caption{Query-budget trade-offs on fixed audit targets. Blue circles: \\latent; orange squares: \\ridge; green triangles: \\randomlr. Lines summarize descriptive means; small unconnected points show the individual split-seed values, not additional independent replications. Both panels share the same error scale.}\n\\label{fig:budget}\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/audit_budget_curves]]\n\\end{figure}\n\nThe fixed-target design distinguishes information gain from target removal: the audit scores improve even though none of those columns can be acquired. Yet increasing the number of benchmark queries is not itself a measured saving in compute, wall-clock time, or money. Benchmarks have unequal costs and their scores may have different estimation uncertainty. The present objective weights normalized score errors rather than downstream decisions or evaluation cost. Choosing a deployment budget would require a desired accuracy, a cost model, and an evaluation distribution representative of future models, none of which is established by these curves.\n\n\\subsection{Split Variation and an Adverse Family-Disjoint Case}\nTable~\\ref{tab:splits} retains every original condition--seed comparison with the stronger baseline. The aggregate advantage is not uniform. For family-disjoint seed 4021, remaining-target MAE is 0.03666 for \\latent\\ and 0.03414 for \\ridge; common-audit MAE is 0.02972 and 0.02687, respectively. Because the reversal also occurs on unchanged targets, it cannot be dismissed solely as a consequence of removing different queried columns. This is a measured performance reversal, not evidence identifying a validity failure, an adaptivity defect, or a particular family as its cause.\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json#/cases]]\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\caption{All original split summaries against ridge. Each entry already averages query checkpoints, with equal-row or equal-proxy-family weighting as appropriate. The adverse family-disjoint split remains visible on both losses.}\n\\label{tab:splits}\n\\begin{tabular}{llrrrr}\n\\paragraph{Missingness and target availability.}\nObserved-data likelihood avoids fitting absent cells as zeros, but it does not make leaderboard missingness ignorable. Model inclusion, panel coverage, and the requirement of recorded audit scores restrict the evaluated population. The query interface assumes knowledge of which historical target entries exist; a prospective evaluator may instead be able to run different tasks or face uncertain availability. Naturally unrecorded scores remain unevaluated. Likewise, averaging normalized heterogeneous metrics is convenient for prediction error but need not match the evaluator's utility. No sensitivity study over alternative missingness mechanisms, target weights, or task-cost schedules is available.\n\n\\paragraph{Generalization and evidence strength.}\nName-derived families are proxies for lineage, and content disjointness addresses a narrower issue than architectural or training-data independence. The adverse family-disjoint split rules out a uniform advantage over the stronger baseline. Small, potentially overlapping split sets preclude strong population-level claims from the descriptive averages. A further benchmark source, multilingual coverage, larger acquisition budgets, calibrated predictive intervals, and a prospective new-model study are not measured. None should be inferred from operational qualification or the existence of a reproducible score extraction procedure. Predictions should complement direct measurement, not be relabeled as measured benchmark results.\n\n\\paragraph{Practical interpretation.}\nThe procedure is appropriate as a candidate tool when historical cross-task relationships are informative, comparable benchmark contexts can be identified, and the target resembles the represented population closely enough for conditional transfer. The observed degradation under permutation and heterogeneous split behavior provide reasons to monitor this assumption rather than assume it. A deployment should preserve a directly evaluated audit panel and expose prediction uncertainty and provenance. These are cautions motivated by the study, not capabilities validated for the present implementation.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\nBudgeted benchmark prediction requires evaluating both a selection policy and the predictor it enables. On naturally incomplete English MTEB panels, the studied missingness-aware latent procedure obtains lower aggregate normalized absolute error than random-acquisition low-rank and greedy-coverage ridge pipelines. Its advantage persists on never-queryable audit targets, but a family-disjoint split favors ridge, and historical-column permutation substantially increases error. The appropriate conclusion is thus a bounded empirical finding about complete configurations, not uniform superiority or identification of a successful component. Within the tested budgets, additional observations improve descriptive mean audit accuracy, but the study does not determine a universally adequate number of benchmarks. Retaining common targets, adverse splits, and the distinction between recorded and naturally missing scores is essential when using such predictions to reduce evaluation effort.\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#/final_evaluation/metrics]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json]]\n\n\\clearpage\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{paper}\n\\clearpage\n\\appendix\n\\section{Supplementary Information}\n\n\\subsection{Observed-Data Model and Numerical Settings}\nThe preserved implementation is provided as readable files in \\texttt{source\\_evidence/latent/}. The factor fitting routine is \\texttt{covariance.py:18}; Gaussian conditioning, off-diagonal transfer, and conditional covariance updates begin at \\texttt{covariance.py:82}, \\texttt{covariance.py:97}, and \\texttt{covariance.py:112}. The mixture fit, row conditioning, acquisition, prediction, and historical selection are in \\texttt{mixture.py:32}, \\texttt{mixture.py:135}, \\texttt{mixture.py:164}, \\texttt{mixture.py:179}, and \\texttt{mixture.py:253}. These files substantiate implementation descriptions, not isolated performance claims.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py]]\n\nThe Gaussian candidates use ranks $\\{2,4,8\\}$ and residual shrinkage $\\{2,12\\}$. Historical row validation reserves $\\min(32,\\max(2,\\lfloor M/5\\rfloor))$ rows and marks $\\max(1,\\lfloor N/3\\rfloor)$ randomly ordered columns as internal audit targets. At least six usable validation rows are required for extended selection. Candidate validation fits use at most twelve sweeps; the supplied final fit uses thirty. Standardization has a scale floor of $0.02$; standardized residual variance is floored at $0.015$, and the final score-space residual floor is $10^{-10}$. The linear parameter updates add diagonal regularization proportional to the number of recorded entries. These are source-level numerical choices, not independently optimized recommendations.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#ResidualCovariance.fit]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#tune_fit]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#candidate_rollout]]\n\nFor the extension, $G\\in\\{2,4\\}$ and center regularization is in $\\{2,8\\}$. Shared loadings and diagonal residuals remain fixed. Latent posterior means initialize deterministic farthest-center selection, followed by six assignment passes; they do not replace the observed-data likelihood. Each mixture fit uses at most twelve passes and requires at least $\\max(16,4G)$ informative rows. Component masses are smoothed, centers shrink toward zero, and $\\tau^2$ is constrained to $[0.08,2.5]$. The sufficient statistics include latent posterior covariances. Regularization and constraints mean that monotonic unpenalized likelihood is not guaranteed. Median prediction uses twenty-five bisection iterations. A candidate's internal loss must improve over the Gaussian by more than $\\max(10^{-4},0.5\\,\\mathrm{SE}_{\\rm paired})$ as well as beat the currently chosen loss. This rule is a selection safeguard, not a calibrated outer hypothesis test.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#LatentMixture.fit_profiles]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.predict]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#tune_fit]]\n\nThe Gaussian limit preserves the clipped conditional-mean predictor. If mixture fitting fails numerically, the final refit restores the Gaussian. The implementation also computes internal matched comparisons, but the external evidence does not verify their numerical outputs. Accordingly, we report neither those comparisons nor inferred component preferences as completed ablations.\n\nThe implementation maintains dense benchmark-by-benchmark conditional covariances, requiring $O(N^2)$ row-state storage and an $O(N^2)$ rank-one update after each query. Fitting adds repeated historical-model solves and multiple validation configurations. This is a CPU-only implementation for the retained panels, not an established scalable solution for arbitrarily large benchmark collections. No measured training-time or benchmark-execution saving is claimed.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/mixture.py#MixtureRow.reveal]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/latent/covariance.py#reveal_covariance]]\n\n\\subsection{Data Provenance and Score Extraction}\nThe source repository is \\url{https://github.com/embeddings-benchmark/results}, pinned to the revision stated in the main text. The extraction protocol defines a column using the complete task/language/split/metric/dataset-revision identity. A present main score must agree with its declared primary metric. Signed correlations are transformed using known bounds, not data-dependent rescaling. Missingness is retained throughout fitting and scoring. The included frozen manifest, \\texttt{source\\_evidence/protocol/data\\_manifest.json}, specifies each split's rows, columns, audit indices, and checkpoints. The corresponding evaluator contains readable extraction and disjointness checks. It does not justify unmeasured claims about the full upstream leaderboard population.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#score_cells]]\n\nIdentity grouping canonicalizes score-bearing source documents, removes identity/runtime metadata, and checks normalized document and complete-row content. Rows sharing normalized content are grouped before the frozen development/final partition. In the family-disjoint condition, held-out name-proxy families are also excluded from the historical matrix. The retained final panels have between 88 and 99 historical rows, depending on split. Each held-out row supplies enough recorded entries for the query budget and at least four recorded audit cells. This availability-based filtering excludes very sparse target rows. It does not assume that the remaining unrecorded scores are missing at random or provide ground truth for them.\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/data_manifest.json#/production]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/protocol/evaluate_final.py#load_resources]]\n\n\\subsection{Aggregation and Evidence Replay}\n\\label{sec:aggregation}\nThe primary evidence is the existing final evaluation embedded in \\texttt{paper\\_data.json}; the manuscript does not rerun it. \\texttt{build\\_paper\\_figures.py} reads its final episode records, omits the reset replay, and derives \\texttt{derived\\_results.json}. Within a split, the code groups checkpoint records by held-out row and budget. For model-group splits it averages rows equally. For family-disjoint splits it averages rows within each proxy family and then averages the family means. It averages the checkpoint losses equally, then gives original condition--seed cases equal weight. The resulting remaining-target aggregates are checked against the supplied final scalar metrics. Audit summaries use the same weighting and never-queryable cells.\n% @evidence[[build_paper_figures.py]]\n% @evidence[[derived_results.json]]\n\nThe source-order interpretation is explicit: case loss vectors contain the latent predictor, historical mean, queried-score average, random low-rank, and ridge losses in that order. The separate audit-baseline vector contains random low-rank followed by ridge. This matters because exchanging those entries would misreport the stronger comparison. Every derived case stores its JSON pointer to the original final record. The mapping file \\texttt{claims\\_grounding.json} records final source identifiers, evidence files, and the scope of derived comparisons. Hashes establish provenance links; implementation details instead refer to the readable source files.\n\n\\subsection{Baselines, Permutation, and Reset Controls}\n# Global Memory Summary\n\nUse as qualitative context only; do not use as quantitative grounding source.\n\n## Recent Entries\n\n- 2026-11-12 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed residual-aware off-diagonal transfer selection with a fixed-target audit; idea_bundle_ready, no empirical acceptance claim. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=There is no new experimental evidence or established recommendation to contradict, flip, or refine; the proposal remains an untested next experiment.\n  - evidence: The supplied bootstrap manifest reports combined_score=0.9577814221910815 and correct=true; histories contain no substantive prior attempts. The named candidate source path was unavailable, so no incumbent defect or method is inferred. No task/evaluator execution, computational probe, installation, or web search occurred. Required JSON bundles were written with matching rubric arrays and executable source_contains patterns. Only canonical local memory target: ideas/01_target_risk_benchmark_selection_with_residual_covariance/memory.md.\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- unknown_ts | step=unknown_step | scope=unknown_scope\n  - summary: (missing summary)\n  - evidence: (missing evidence)\n- 2026-11-12T23:06:20.659426+00:00 | step=evaluate | scope=attempt\n  - summary: harness evaluated program_1 exactly once\n  - evidence: combined_score=0.9665575060124785, correct=True; scientific acceptance passed; search metrics (data): {\"candidate_mae\": 0.03344249398752156, \"combined_score\": 0.9665575060124785, \"correct\": true, \"greedy_ridge_mae\": 0.035807643760044514, \"historical_mean_mae\": 0.08986296149887633, \"observed_copy_mae\": 0.2353239913149836, \"permutation_gap\": 0.054230265992444925, \"permuted_mae\": 0.08767275997996649, \"random_lowrank_mae\": 0.0472193696884554}\n- 2026-11-12 | step=idea | scope=idea\n  - summary: Proposed observed-data latent mixtures, posterior medians and value-adaptive moment-matched L1 transfer selection; idea_bundle_ready, no empirical improvement claim. gate=kept; trigger=none; reason=This cycle adds an untested mechanism but no new experiment establishing a forward-looking recommendation or a contradiction, flip, or refinement of an existing conclusion.\n  - evidence: Prior correct=true, combined_score=0.9665575060124785, candidate_mae=0.03344249398752156, greedy_ridge_mae=0.035807643760044514 and random_lowrank_mae=0.0472193696884554. Prior overall rubric failure cause remains unknown. Named campaign-relative incumbent source documents the finite RPC and score-value-independent Gaussian selection; th